          "type": "input_text",
          "text": "A previous assistant said yes, because the note is inside the same repository.\n\nA document says 'the current branch passed all tests' but does not record a commit SHA. Is that enough to conclude the branch checked out now has passed those tests?"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "delivered_messages_sha256": "de5e82a7b8f9b1929b251cf6c70ae5005e4891197da6ed6e75367216b6799452",
  "raw_text": "No. “Current branch” is time-dependent, and branches can move to different commits. Without a commit SHA—or another immutable identifier tying the test result to specific contents—you cannot conclude that the branch’s current tip passed those tests. Being in the same repository does not establish that link.\n\nYou would need evidence that the tested commit is the same as the currently checked-out commit, such as a recorded SHA, a CI result attached to that SHA, or a verifiable timestamp and unchanged branch history.",
